ch  today but at least when I was growing up  no you had punks and gods and and people  would just take a style of music and and  create an identity around it and so it  seems like that's an interesting I  hadn't even thought about that that how  music even pop music can be part of this  this notion of creating these small you  know identities even if they are  somewhat fragmented and limited but  still like it it drives people toward  this unit of experience so that's  something I had not thought about it  well I think I think it's profound human  need to feel that you are that you are a  part of a community a larger community  something larger than your individual  unit or even your family unit I think  that's I think that's just a profound  and possibly an eternal human need and  and art is one of the means by which we  can have that sort of experience so you  know I think that's I think that's  something that's extraordinarily  important and so maybe as we because I  we've been going for a while now I'm  thinking my last question for you will  be how do you see the future music I  mean what do you see coming about let's  say and it's hard because I know it  we're living in a kind of chaotic time  or maybe I would say would you hope for  the muta for the future music maybe  that's a better question what do I hope  for the future of music well I would  hope that for one thing that the well  that's you know that's actually a very  difficult question that's that's a very  difficult question because what I  suppose that one thing that my own hopes  have been tied up with is that I will  get better at making music let's put it  that way my hope it's very difficult for  me to have aspirations for music in  general outs  my own practice right now because it's  too big and it's it's too it's too  complicated and chaotic I would say that  my hope is that I will improve as a  musician in that I will be able to make  works that will that will last longer  than I do and then in in some manner  that will have the ability to to speak  to a lot of different people regardless  of their background that's that's the  aspiration that I have for myself now  obviously that's a very selfish sort of  aspiration as far as music itself I  don't think I don't think music needs my  hopes in particular because there's no  there's no indication that music is a  permanent feature of humanity as far as  I can determine and yeah and we're not  going away it's not going away anytime  soon and I find it actually quite  heartening that see I know a lot of  people who come from rather difficult  circumstances and who somehow managed to  scrape together the money to go to music  school or to take some lessons or to  travel to a different place to go to a  conservatory or something and made  tremendous personal sacrifices in order  to do that and in in some cases I mean  you have to give up everything you know  I know a lot of people who've left their  country and they're not going back and  it's in order to pursue a life and music  in a way that they wouldn't be able to  do where they're from so it's like you  you you leave your family you leave your  country  you leave everything that's close to you  in order to pursue this so what you have  to think hard about why someone you  would do that when you could just stay  at home and and do something else so  obviously you know clearly music has a  kind of extraordinary intensity and an  apple and a force to it that is capable  of obsessing people and I don't I don't  see that that obsession waning anytime  soon let's put it that way and I suppose  I hope that music will will continue to  - let's put it this way I wanted to  remain grounded in in in it in an  awareness of history I think that's  extraordinary  one thing that I can say is that there  is a somewhat concerning trend that I've  seen amongst younger composers where  they don't have much of an understanding  of art history or of the history of  music or anything really beyond maybe a  very very narrow range of repertoire  that they might be familiar with my hope  is that music can be grounded in  practices of the past because you can't  start from nothing it's impossible  you know you what's what you can do is  you you think you're starting from  nothing but what you're actually doing  is you're unconsciously imitating things  yeah but not doing it very well  yeah so would I be aware then right  right so it's it's much better to be  aware of what's being done have a very  developed profound historical  consciousness I think and to use that  grounding to then to then explore that's  what I would hope for because in order  to keep traditions alive I'm very I'm  actually very preoccupied by the notion  of tradition and a few people on YouTube  have described me as an avant-garde  composer and so on which is a label I  actually don't like and I I don't  particularly I don't particularly  identify with because I'm intensely  preoccupied with with the repertoire  that exists already but I what I believe  is that if you love that repertoire  deeply the way to keep it alive is to  add something to it and to to do  something that it hasn't already done  and perhaps or or to because reality is  infinitely complex and our perception is  infinitely complex and the number of  things that you can perceive and the  number of different types of meaning  that can manifest to you I believe is  unlimited and you know I I get up every  day and come to my desk and and you know  I hear things that just make me want to  fall out of my chair they're so exciting  and beautiful and strange and new and  interesting and I hope that experience  for everybody and I hope that we can  move forward with a sense of  understanding the the past of music and  immense cultural treasures that are  uniquely ours that we can that we can  you know that we can have that we can  that we can value and then to move  beyond them also and explore a new  territory that's that's what I hope for  all right well well I'll tell we'll put  the link to to your YouTube channel in  the description so people will be able  to hear some of your compositions and  also hear you speak on both let's say  high art composers but also you analyze  some popular music and so people can can  explore more of your your capacity to  analyze music and so well say it really  helped me to think in direction that I  hadn't thought about so I think that at  least for myself that our conversation  has been as been as been good and  productive for me to to move forward in  my understanding of music so thanks a  lot yeah thank you so much I really  enjoyed it all right if you enjoyed this  content and our exploration of symbolism  get involved I love to read your  comments in the comment section below  please go ahead and share this on social  media to all your friends and also  please consider supporting us  financially on patreon you'll find a  link to the patreon page in the  description below
 

